## Why This Is Asked
Tests ownership, bias for action, and customer-obsession in a live scenario where risk management matters. It reveals how a junior engineer steps up, triages quickly, and communicates clearly under pressure.

## Key Concepts
- Ownership mindset when a customer-visible issue arises
- Bias for action: fast decision to patch vs rollback
- Customer-focused communication and transparency
- Incident triage, rollback strategy, and postmortem learnings
- Cross-functional coordination with support and product

## Code Example
```javascript
// Safe feature flag rollout with quick rollback
let featureEnabled = false;
try {
  enableFunctionFlag();
  featureEnabled = true;
} catch (e) {
  featureEnabled = false;
  rollbackFeature();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle external stakeholders who push for speed at the cost of reliability?
- What metrics would you monitor to confirm rollback success and minimized customer impact?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes real-time risk handling, cross-functional negotiation, and release engineering under regulatory pressure.

## Key Concepts

- Incident risk assessment and decision framing
- Canary releases and feature flags
- Rollback plans and rollback testing
- Privacy, regulatory compliance, and data governance
- Stakeholder alignment and concise communication

## Code Example

```javascript
// gated rollout example
if (featureFlags.analyticsEnabled && privacy.isCompliant()) {
  enableAnalytics();
} else {
  enableFallbackAnalytics();
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure success of the chosen path?
- What changes would you make to incident playbooks to cover similar risks in the future?

